/// A cell in the grid. You can use this function in the argument list of a grid to
/// override grid style properties for an individual cell or manually positioning it
/// within the grid. You can also use this function in show rules to apply certain
/// styles to multiple cells at once.
///
/// For example, you can override the position and stroke for a single cell:
///
/// You may also apply a show rule on `grid.cell` to style all cells at once, which
/// allows you, for example, to apply styles based on a cell’s position. Refer to the
/// examples of the `table.cell` element to learn more about this.
///
/// #quote(block: true)[*Note:* In HTML, the body sits in the cell's inner block, and
/// `colspan`, `rowspan`, `inset`, `align`, `fill`, `stroke`, and `breakable` become
/// CSS. See docs/html-semantics.md for every field. Paged output is unchanged.]
///
/// - body (content): The cell’s body.
///
///   This parameter is positional. \
///   #quote(block: true)[*Note:* In HTML, selectable content in the cell's inner block;
///   nested supported layouts remain HTML.]
///
/// - x (auto, int): The cell’s column (zero-indexed). This field may be used in show
///   rules to style a cell depending on its column.
///
///   You may override this field to pick in which column the cell must be placed. If no
///   row (`y`) is chosen, the cell will be placed in the first row (starting at row 0)
///   with that column available (or a new row if none). If both `x` and `y` are chosen,
///   however, the cell will be placed in that exact position. An error is raised if
///   that position is not available (thus, it is usually wise to specify cells with a
///   custom position before cells with automatic positions).
///
///   Default: `auto`. \
///   #quote(block: true)[*Note:* In HTML, the logical column, counted from the start
///   side of the text direction. `auto` searches for a free column.]
///
/// - y (auto, int): The cell’s row (zero-indexed). This field may be used in show rules
///   to style a cell depending on its row.
///
///   You may override this field to pick in which row the cell must be placed. If no
///   column (`x`) is chosen, the cell will be placed in the first column (starting at
///   column 0) available in the chosen row. If all columns in the chosen row are
///   already occupied, an error is raised.
///
///   Default: `auto`. \
///   #quote(block: true)[*Note:* In HTML, the row. `auto` searches for a free row.]
///
/// - colspan (int): The amount of columns spanned by this cell.
///
///   Default: `1`. \
///   #quote(block: true)[*Note:* In HTML, the number of columns spanned, including
///   gutter tracks in the CSS span.]
///
/// - rowspan (int): The amount of rows spanned by this cell.
///
///   Default: `1`. \
///   #quote(block: true)[*Note:* In HTML, the number of rows spanned, including gutter
///   tracks. Creates extra rows when needed.]
///
/// - inset (auto, relative, dictionary): The cell’s inset override.
///
///   Default: `auto`. \
///   #quote(block: true)[*Note:* In HTML, CSS padding. `auto` inherits the grid inset;
///   `0pt` removes padding.]
///
/// - align (auto, alignment): The cell’s alignment override.
///
///   Default: `auto`. \
///   #quote(block: true)[*Note:* In HTML, the cell's alignment. `auto` inherits the
///   grid's value.]
///
/// - fill (none, auto, color, gradient, tiling): The cell’s fill override.
///
///   Default: `auto`. \
///   #quote(block: true)[*Note:* In HTML, the CSS background. `auto` inherits the grid
///   fill, and `none` removes it.]
///
/// - stroke (none, length, color, gradient, stroke, tiling, dictionary):
///   The cell’s stroke override.
///
///   Default: `(:)`. \
